  <prompt>Today the client picks a container and the server obeys. Invert it: upload routes take the owning record, the server resolves the container from the record it is already authorizing, and the client's container-picking code is deleted rather than corrected.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE full-stack engineer. The deliverable is a contract change proven complete by DELETION — no client supplies a container to an upload route afterward, and a grep proves it.</role>

  <goal>Upload routes are keyed on `(entity, recordId)`. The server resolves the container through task 075's resolver, from the same record it authorizes. No client code decides which container to upload into. The creation wizard no longer writes `sprk_containerid`. Task 074's three Pending OBO upload waivers are deleted because the routes are gated, not because the rule was relaxed.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>⭐ **READ THIS FIRST — THIS TASK WAS REWRITTEN. Options (A) and (B) are CLOSED. Do not re-open them.**

The escalation note recommended **(A)** — keep the client resolving a BU container at wizard-open as a *fallback*, and add a resolver call at each upload point. The owner chose **(C)**. The reasoning, which you should not re-litigate:

- **The decisive argument is not scope; it is one key versus two.** Under (A) the authorization decision keys on `(entity, recordId)` while the container still comes from a client-supplied value — **two keys for one decision.** Finding F-9 proves they already diverge in shipped code: `CreateProjectWizard` uploads into the container resolved when the wizard *opened*, while provisioning's correct stamp is discarded. Under (C) the authorization key and the container are the same value by construction and **cannot** disagree.
- **(C) removes the SHAPE that made 073's vulnerability expressible.** `OBOEndpoints.cs:51` is the same "write into a caller-named container" shape 073 deletes from the app-only twin; it survives only because it runs under OBO rather than app-only.
- **(C) fixes the highest-value site by deleting it.** F-5 (`AssociateToStep.tsx:154-160`) is an already-record-aware resolver that **fails OPEN** — reads the record's `sprk_containerid`, falls through to the BU container on *any* failure. Under (A) it becomes a corrected call site you must keep correct forever. Under (C) it stops existing.
- **The client-side comparison inverts.** (A) *adds* a resolver call at ~9 sites and *retains* `speContainerIdRef` as a fallback. (C) *removes* the container parameter. Deletion, not addition.
- **The escalation note's scope objection was already stale when written** — see §1.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§1 — THE SERVER SURFACE, RE-MEASURED 2026-08-27. It is one route converted and two deleted.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

The escalation note called (C) *"not deliverable inside 076 as scoped"* because it *"spans task 073's authorization + this task's routing + the OBO upload trio's waivers."* **That framing predates task 073 shipping its deletion.** Verified on this branch today:

| Container-keyed route | Verified state | 076's work |
|---|---|---|
| `PUT /api/containers/{containerId}/files/{*path}` (`UploadEndpoints.cs:24`) | **073 deletes the file entirely** — 218 lines, zero additions | none |
| `POST /api/containers/{containerId}/upload` (`UploadEndpoints.cs:113`) | same | none |
| `PUT /api/upload-session/chunk` (`UploadEndpoints.cs:164`) | same | none |
| `PUT /api/obo/containers/{id}/files/{*path}` (`OBOEndpoints.cs:51`) | **THE live client upload route** — 11 call sites per 074's waiver | ✅ **convert** |
| `POST /api/obo/drives/{driveId}/upload-session` (`OBOEndpoints.cs:102`) | **unreachable — see below** | ✅ **delete** |
| `PUT /api/obo/upload-session/chunk` (`OBOEndpoints.cs:137`) | **unreachable — see below** | ✅ **delete** |
| `GET /api/v1/containers/{containerId}/documents` (`DataverseDocumentsEndpoints.cs:533`) | **already task 078**, which already depends on 075 | out of scope |
| `SpeAdmin/Container*Endpoints.cs` (12 routes) | administer the **container itself**; there is no owning record to key on | out of scope |

**The chunked OBO pair is dead, and the right treatment is DELETE not CONVERT — verify this before designing anything.** Its only client, `Spaarke.SdapClient/src/operations/UploadOperation.ts:94-110`, first calls `GET /api/obo/containers/{containerId}/drive` at `:98` to obtain a drive id. **That route is mapped NOWHERE** — verified 2026-08-27; the only occurrences of the string in the BFF are two comments (`DocumentsEndpoints.cs:16`, `SpeFileStoreDtos.cs:15`). So `createUploadSession` throws `'Failed to get container drive'` before it ever reaches `:110`. Task 074's own waiver text says the same thing independently. Confirm it yourself, then delete the pair and its client rather than giving a dead path a new contract.

Related and NOT a contradiction: the merge plan's follow-up #2 reports `CreateUploadSessionAsync` / `UploadChunkAsync` as having zero production callers. Those are the **app-only** `SpeFileStore` methods (`SpeFileStore.cs:86/92`), whose only caller is `UploadEndpoints.cs:140` — which 073 deletes. The **OBO** variants (`*AsUserAsync`, `SpeFileStore.cs:288/296`) are live via `OBOEndpoints.cs:119/172`. Deleting the two OBO routes makes BOTH pairs dead; say so in the notes and hand the `UploadSessionManager` cleanup to that follow-up rather than doing it here.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§2 — THE CLIENT SURFACE. Three upload clients; twelve container suppliers.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

The escalation note's inventory (`notes/task-076-callsite-inventory-and-ESCALATION.md` §3) is complete and is your starting point — it corrected the original POML from "7 client sites" to **12 resolution sites + 2 write-back sites + 9 server sites**. Re-grep anyway; every count asserted in this project has been wrong at least once.

**The structure (C) exposes, which (A) obscured**: those 12 sites do not each upload. They **supply a container to one of three upload clients**:

| # | Upload client | Route it calls | Verified |
|---|---|---|---|
| U1 | `Spaarke.UI.Components/src/services/EntityCreationService.ts:493` (`uploadFilesToSpe`) | `PUT /api/obo/containers/{containerId}/files/{fileName}` | 2026-08-27 |
| U2 | `Spaarke.UI.Components/src/services/document-upload/SdapApiClient.ts:101` (`uploadFile`) | same | 2026-08-27 |
| U3 | `Spaarke.SdapClient/src/operations/UploadOperation.ts:27` | same | 2026-08-27 |
| — | `UploadOperation.ts:110` (chunked) | dead — see §1 | 2026-08-27 |

So the client cutover is **three signatures**, and the 12 suppliers then have nothing to supply. That is why (C) is smaller than (A) on the client, not larger.

**Classify each of the 12, do not blanket-delete.** A supplier that exists only to feed U1/U2/U3 is deleted. A supplier that feeds a **non-upload** consumer is NOT this task's to remove — it belongs to whoever owns that read:

- `SemanticSearchControl/.../NavigationService.ts:354-362` builds a user→BU→container chain and passes `&containerId=` **on a navigation URL**, not an upload.
- `LegalWorkspace/.../WorkspaceGrid.tsx:535-537` and `src/client/webresources/js/sprk_analysis_commands.js:58` likewise choose a container for a **read/command**, not bytes.

For each, write one line: *deleted (fed only uploads)* / *retained, owned by task 078* / *retained, needs a new task — filed as X*. **A supplier left unclassified is a survivor**, and survivors are what this task exists to eliminate.

**Two harmful writes — delete both, unchanged from the original scope:**

- **W1** `Spaarke.UI.Components/src/services/EntityCreationService.ts:327` (`applyDefaultContainerId`, via `applyUserBuDefaults:374`) stamps the acting user's BU container onto the new row — **including secure projects**. Verified independently: `CreateProjectWizard/projectService.ts:283-285` sets only `sprk_issecure`, then `:290-293` calls `applyUserBuDefaults` **unconditionally**, with no suppression.
- **W2** `src/solutions/DocumentUploadWizard/sprk_subgrid_commands.js` overwrites `sprk_project.sprk_containerid` with the BU container when the field is not on the form — defeating a correct resolver *after the fact*.

**An id-space smell to classify, not assume** (compose-r8's second finding class — caller-id vs other-id passed without translation): `SdapApiClient.uploadFile` interpolates `request.driveId` into the `{containerId}` route slot (`:101`) while logging it as `containerId: request.driveId` (`:95`), and the route handler then calls `ResolveDriveIdAsync(id)` on it (`OBOEndpoints.cs:65-67`). Either the field name is wrong or the value is. Determine which before the cutover — under (C) both ids leave the client, so this resolves by deletion, but the answer tells you whether U2 was ever actually working.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§3 — THE SERVER-SIDE ARCHIVE PATH (strategy 2). Unchanged by A-vs-C; still 076's.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

Seven sites remain after 075 routed two. **This is the one easiest to forget** — no client, no wizard, no UI. If missed, secure correspondence lands in the shared archive container.

| File : line | Status |
|---|---|
| `Services/Communication/IncomingCommunicationProcessor.cs:868` (attachments) | ✅ routed in 075 |
| `Services/Communication/IncomingCommunicationProcessor.cs:991` (`.eml`) | ✅ routed in 075 |
| `Services/Communication/CommunicationService.cs:460 / 1259 / 1574 / 2054 / 2146` | 🔲 **076** |
| `Services/Communication/MessageAttachmentMaterializer.cs:114` | 🔲 **076** |
| `Services/Communication/CommunicationService.cs:2368` | ⚠️ its comment claims the legacy path "is no longer used here" while five siblings still read it — **do not trust the comment; verify** |

The mechanism is already built and proven in 075: `CommunicationContainerResolver` + `ResolveContainerForContentAsync`, which resolves each site's regarding context itself.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§4 — WHY COMPLETENESS IS STILL THE DELIVERABLE, and why (C) makes it cheaper to prove
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

SharePoint Embedded permissions are **additive-only**. A single missed path puts secure content in a shared container **permanently** — no later permission change retracts it. Partial completion is not partial credit.

But (C) changes what the proof looks like, and this is the practical payoff. Under (A) the proof is *"every one of N sites calls the resolver"* — an assertion about behaviour that has to be re-proven whenever anyone adds a site. Under (C) the proof is *"no client supplies a container to an upload route"* — a **grep for absence**, and a new site cannot regress it because the parameter is gone from the contract.

**Zero secure projects exist in any environment** (`TASK-INDEX.md:110`, re-verified), so no bytes are misplaced today and there is nothing to migrate. **The window closes on FIRST USE, not on a date** — which makes this more urgent to land, not less. State that explicitly wherever you cite F-9, or a reader checks App Insights, finds nothing, and downgrades a live-but-unfired defect to a non-issue.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§5 — DEPLOY ORDERING. Two obligations (C) creates that (A) did not.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

1. **Client and BFF must ship together.** The upload contract changes on both sides. A BFF deployed ahead of the client 404s every upload; a client deployed ahead of the BFF does the same. Say this in the PR description and in the notes — it is the single most likely way this task causes an outage.
2. **075 must be merged first** (the resolver), and **073 first** (it deletes `UploadEndpoints.cs`, removing the app-only twin from the surface you are reshaping).

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
§6 — WHAT (C) DOES TO TASK 074'S WAIVERS. This is the forcing-function payoff.
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

`tests/Spaarke.ArchTests/RouteAuthorizationGuardTests.cs` carries three `Pending` waivers owned by `"073/075/076"` (`:235`, `:240`, `:244`). All three **disappear**: one route becomes gated, two are deleted. `NoWaiverIsStale` fails the build if you gate a route and leave its waiver behind, so this is enforced, not remembered.

The waiver at `:235` states the constraint that (C) satisfies, in its own words: *"Attaching `DocumentAuthorizationFilter` makes `ExtractResourceId` hand back a CONTAINER id, which is not an `sprk_documents` GUID, so `RetrievePrincipalAccess` returns None and 100% of uploads deny."* Under (C) the route hands back `(entity, recordId)` and the decision becomes answerable.

**Observation, NOT scope**: `POST /api/v1/documents` carries a **Permanent** waiver reading *"CREATE. There is no pre-existing resource to authorize"* (`:268`) — the same reasoning as the OBO upload waiver you are about to delete. Under (C) that reasoning weakens for the byte-upload but the row-create waiver is untouched here. Note it for the wrap-up; do not act on it.</background>
