  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Verify the three §1 facts on the merged branch before designing: `UploadEndpoints.cs` is gone (073); 075's resolver is present; `GET /api/obo/containers/{id}/drive` is mapped nowhere. If any is false, the plan's shape changed — say so before proceeding.</step>
    <step order="1">Design the route contract and write it down first: the literal shape carrying `(entity, recordId)`, which filter makes the decision, how 075's resolver is reached from it, and what a secure record with no container returns to the client. One paragraph, in the notes, before code.</step>
    <step order="2">Convert `PUT /api/obo/containers/{id}/files/{*path}` to the record-keyed contract and attach the authorization filter.</step>
    <step order="3">Delete the chunked OBO pair (`OBOEndpoints.cs:102/137`) and its dead client (`UploadOperation.ts:94-122`).</step>
    <step order="4">Cut the three client upload clients (U1/U2/U3) over to the new contract — signatures take `(entity, recordId)`, not a container.</step>
    <step order="5">Classify all 12 container suppliers: delete the upload-only ones; for each retained one write the owning task. Unclassified = survivor.</step>
    <step order="6">Delete W1 and W2.</step>
    <step order="7">Route the seven remaining server-side Communication sites (§3) through 075's `CommunicationContainerResolver`.</step>
    <step order="8">Delete the three `Pending` OBO waivers in `RouteAuthorizationGuardTests.cs`.</step>
    <step order="9">Tests that FAIL without the change: an upload against a secure record lands in that record's own container; an upload against a secure record with no container FAILS rather than falling back; a non-secure record is unchanged; a caller without access to the record is DENIED (the negative case — this route was ungated before, so it is the one with no prior coverage at all). Perturbation-check at least the deny case: break the gate, confirm a test goes red.</step>
    <step order="10">Re-grep for absence — no client supplies a container to an upload route, no `sprk_containerid` client write survives. This grep IS the acceptance evidence.</step>
    <step order="11">Build (`build:prod` for PCF), full test run, publish size, CVE. Record the contract, the ship-together obligation, and the classification table in notes.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If an upload path genuinely has NO owning record at the moment the bytes move — so `(entity, recordId)` cannot be supplied — STOP and surface it. That is the modelling gap option (C) exists to close; if one survives, the contract needs a different shape (a server-issued upload ticket, say), and that is an owner decision, not an implementation detail. Do NOT reintroduce a client-supplied container "just for that one path" — that is option (B) arriving through the back door, and (B) was rejected.</trigger>
    <trigger>If converting the route forces a second authorization mechanism because `EntityAccessFilter` / `CallerRecordAccessProbe` cannot serve the route shape, STOP. Two mechanisms deciding one thing is the failure class every finding in this project came from.</trigger>
    <trigger>If `GET /api/obo/containers/{id}/drive` turns out to be reachable after all (a proxy, a rewrite, a deployed web resource), the chunked pair is LIVE, not dead — stop and re-plan §1 rather than deleting a live upload path.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>075 provides the resolver and its reverse mapping. `EntityAccessFilter` + `CallerRecordAccessProbe` already authorize `(entityType, recordId)` for the caller. The upload routes, the three client upload clients, and the BU cascade all exist.</existing>
    <extension>This is a contract change plus deletions on existing surface: one route reshaped, two deleted, three client signatures changed, two harmful writes and ~9 container suppliers removed. The only genuinely new thing is the filter wiring on the converted route, and that extends an existing filter rather than adding one.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Concrete, named, and currently live: `PUT /api/obo/containers/{id}/files/{*path}` accepts a caller-named container with no per-resource decision, and `AssociateToStep.tsx:154-160` silently redirects secure content to the shared BU container on any read failure. Because SPE permissions are additive-only, the first secure project created with files puts those bytes in shared storage permanently — the window closes on first use, not on a date.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

