  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>The upload route takes `(entity, recordId)` and resolves its container server-side through 075's resolver. No upload route accepts a caller-named container.</criterion>
    <criterion>The upload route carries a per-resource authorization decision, and a caller without access to the owning record is denied — proven by a test that fails without the gate.</criterion>
    <criterion>An upload against a secure record lands in that record's own container; against a secure record with a null container it FAILS rather than falling back — both proven by tests.</criterion>
    <criterion>Non-secure upload behaviour is unchanged.</criterion>
    <criterion>A grep proves NO client code supplies a container to an upload route, and no client `sprk_containerid` write survives. This absence-grep is the completeness evidence.</criterion>
    <criterion>All 12 container suppliers are classified in the notes — deleted, or retained with the owning task named. None unclassified.</criterion>
    <criterion>The chunked OBO pair is deleted, with the `GET /api/obo/containers/{id}/drive` 404 verified first-hand rather than taken from this POML.</criterion>
    <criterion>The seven server-side Communication sites are routed (the ones with no client and no UI — verify explicitly).</criterion>
    <criterion>`RouteAuthorizationGuardTests.cs` has zero `Pending` waivers owned by 073/075/076, and none were converted to `Permanent`.</criterion>
    <criterion>The client+BFF ship-together obligation is stated in the PR description and the notes.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Completeness is still the entire deliverable — one missed path is a permanent isolation failure, because SPE cannot retract it. What option (C) changes is the *proof*: it is an argument from absence (no client names a container) rather than an argument from behaviour (every site calls the resolver), and absence is the one a future contributor cannot silently regress. Grep, do not remember.</notes>
</task>
